\p{Profiler "P10", 2026-11-24, 22:15Z to 23:50Z. The project owner's question, on the two warm wall clocks of one pass over all of Mathlib with the machine engine and 8 workers (\code{\verb~|work/status/machine-integration.md~}, wave 6):}

\blockquote{
\p{"27.4 s (21.2 s on the JVM): do you have itemized cost: gc, hash consing, num computation, etc."}
}

\p{This report is that itemization: ONE table (section 0), in seconds of wall clock and in CPU seconds, for the JVM launcher and for the native image, every sampled method assigned to exactly one item by a published file (\code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/items.map~}), so that the table can be re-made after the next change (section 7). No code was changed or published. Living summary: \code{\verb~|work/status/prof-P10.md~}.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\p{Same export (\code{\verb~|work/exports/mathlib.ndjson~}, 6.33 GB, 721,845 units), same jars (published 22:18Z; machine \code{\verb~|df36be3c8cc9~}), \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} with its default switch set, 8 workers, load pipelined with checking, a warm pass of \code{\verb~|-X passes=N~} on the 8 quiet measurement cores. Today's numbers for the two clocks of the question: \strong{21.93 s on the JVM launcher} (164.1 s of process CPU; passes 33.41, 21.93, 23.27 s) and \strong{26.48 s in the native image} (192.4 s of CPU; passes 27.36, 27.04, 26.48 s).}

\p{\strong{How to read it.} A CPU second of a thread costs an eighth of a second of wall clock (8 cores, all busy), so every line has both; a collection pause and a core that the process did not get count as they are. The level-1 lines add up to the wall clock; the level-2 lines add up to the workers' line. Shares of the samples of a profiled pass, seconds of an unprofiled pass of the same process (section 1).}

