\p{What the table says, in the owner's words:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{gc.} JVM launcher: 26 young collections per pass free 14.9 GB; 0.56 s of pauses in which all 8 workers stand still while the collector thread works (0.58 s of CPU): \strong{2.6 \% of the pass}. Native image: 121 collections per pass free 20.9 GB; 2.18 s of pauses, \strong{8.2 \% of the pass} - and the image pays for memory a second time in the kernel: 7.7 s of kernel CPU for 6.3 million page faults per pass, 1.0 s of wall clock (the JVM: 0.44 s; it touches its 13 GB heap once and re-uses a 1 GB nursery). Memory management is 2.3 s of the 4.55 s between the two platforms (section 3.3); the rest is that C2's code for the workers needs 12 \% less CPU than the image's.}
\li{\strong{hash consing.} The largest item on both platforms: \strong{27.6 \% of the workers' CPU on the JVM (40.9 s CPU, 5.1 s of the 21.9 s), 25.4 \% in the image (44.5 s, 5.6 s of the 26.5 s)}. Four fifths of it are PROBES that end in a hit or in a miss (closures and tabling records 9.7 s, neutral applications 11.1 s, frames 6.2 s, constants and sorts 5.6 s on the JVM), 1.5 s hashing, 4.1 s writing the new records and runs, 2.8 s growing and re-hashing tables (89 \% of that asked for by \code{\verb~|Machine.addRecordAt~}). It is NOT a cache-miss item as a whole: in the image its probes of closures, frames and neutral applications run at 4.2-4.4 instructions per cycle with average miss rates; the cache-miss items are table growth (IPC 2.1; 11 L1 misses per 1,000 instructions), the pair table, the \strong{universe-level stores (IPC 1.8, half of their cycles stalled on memory, 12 \% of all last-level misses for 3 \% of the instructions)} and the kernel (section 3.2).}
\li{\strong{num computation.} \strong{0.1 \% of the workers' CPU on the JVM (0.15 s CPU, 0.02 s of wall clock), 0.2 \% in the image (0.39 s).} Nat folding is on the stack of 0.17 \% of the samples. Mathlib does not compute: 273,045 Nat folds and 15,835 offset steps among 936.8 million judgment calls of a pass (statistics of the same jar).}
\li{\p{\strong{etc. - what is large and was not asked for by name:}}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance is 0.12 \% as code and 27.5 \% as a context} (JVM: 40.8 s CPU, 5.1 s of wall clock with \code{\verb~|Conv.pirr~} on the host stack): the type inference, evaluation and comparison of types that the official proof-comparison rule triggers. The two shortcuts remove it: with them the workers need 105.3 s instead of 148.3 s and the pass 15.71 s instead of 21.93 s (image: 20.40 instead of 27.20 s; section 4); what disappears is hash consing (-18.8 s), evaluation and code blocks (-12.4 s), memo lookups (-7.5 s).}
\li{\strong{The reference-engine fallback for the 6,799 units (0.94 \%) the machine declines (6,711 at eager reduction, 88 at other limits) is 12.5 \% of the workers' CPU} (JVM 18.6 s, image 19.4 s; 2.3-2.4 s of wall clock), 58 \% of it term-level substitution (\code{\verb~|TermOps~}, \code{\verb~|Overlay~}). The switch \code{\verb~|eager~} removes it: 18.07 s instead of 21.93 s (image: 24.18 instead of 27.20 s).}
\li{\strong{Universe levels are 7.9 \% (11.7 s CPU on the JVM, 10.9 s in the image)}: instantiating the level list of every constant that is evaluated and interning the result in the shared level store.}
\li{\strong{Memo lookups that are not interning are 11.4 \% (16.8 s)}, half of them the inline caches and block lookup of code blocks (7.8 s), then the pair table (4.7 s) and the tabling gate of EVAL (3.7 s).}
\li{\strong{Reading the export's term nodes is 7.2 \% (10.6 s)} on the JVM, where the readers' inlined frames are exact: application spines are walked node by node (\code{\verb~|Terms.spineLength~}, \code{\verb~|spine~}, \code{\verb~|kind~}).}
\li{\strong{The load is not free in a pipelined pass}: loader thread, two scanner threads and the environment thread use 13.1 s of CPU on the same 8 cores (1.6 s of wall clock, 7.5 \%).}
\li{Sessions (resets 0.14 \%, slab growth 0.34 \%), the driver (0.11 \%), the worklist loop (0.02 \%), admission on the worker (0.04 \%), the JIT in a warm pass (0.1-1.2 \%), deoptimisation (2 events per pass) and safepoints other than collections (none) are below 1 \% each on the JVM.}
}}
\li{\strong{Against gruszka} (12.5 s wall, 75.6 s CPU with 8 threads on the same cores, \code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~}): our 164.1 s of CPU are 88.5 s more. With \code{\verb~|eager~} and the two shortcuts (section 4, configuration (c)) the JVM launcher's pass is \strong{13.05 s and 100.7 s of CPU} (image: 17.16 s, 124.2 s): 0.6 s and 25 s away. So the difference is, in this order: the proof comparison (43 s of worker CPU), the fallback (17-21 s), and then 25 s that remain after both, in which the loader side (12.8 s) and universe levels (9.7 s) are the largest lines that are not checking proper.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 Binaries and configuration}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Distribution} \code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/dist~}, built 2026-11-24T22:18Z with \code{\verb~|scripts/build.sh --published --no-pgo --dist work/prof/P10/dist~} (JVM launcher \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm-jvm~} + CE native image \code{\verb~|bin/rlvm~}, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, \code{\verb~|-R:MaxHeapSize=13g~}; GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3, JDK 25.0.5; no Oracle GraalVM). Jars (sha256 prefix, publication): rlvm-machine \code{\verb~|df36be3c8cc9~} (22:12Z: E1's typed bindings of 21:26Z, the switch \code{\verb~|eager~} of 21:58Z, V1's F-V1-M2), rlvm-driver \code{\verb~|31fa08d9ce61~}, rlvm-load \code{\verb~|bb5734f612c3~}, rlvm-store \code{\verb~|5b72707013bc~}, rlvm-export \code{\verb~|14faa521df15~}, rlvm-admit \code{\verb~|398a117d4ad8~}, rlvm-inductive \code{\verb~|c15b14fffdd3~}, rlvm-ref \code{\verb~|ca1f9bd918bb~}, rlvm-level \code{\verb~|75bb0db242c5~}, rlvm-nat \code{\verb~|b1dda7562adb~}, rlvm-api \code{\verb~|6ff40825ca1d~}, rlvm-cli \code{\verb~|f2af2657a704~}, rlvm-truffle \code{\verb~|ad3256d0751d~}, rlvm-main \code{\verb~|9995ff8855ab~} (compiled from the tree at 22:18Z). \code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/dist-sym~}: the same jar bytes and image flags plus \code{\verb~|-g~} (DWARF with inlined frames; \code{\verb~|.text~} is 17 KB of 41 MB larger): the image \code{\verb~|perf~} samples are taken from. No time in this report is from it unless said.}
\li{\strong{Engine.} \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} with its default switch set (\code{\verb~|Op.SW_DEFAULT~} of that jar; the statistics name it): \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect,bodies,pchk,match,joint,code,casetrees,lazycases,directrules,lazyeval,same~}; host depth 128; machine session cap 1 MiB, driver session threshold 4 MiB. Level 3: \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,eager~}; \code{\verb~|default,facts,probes~} with \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs~} (the two proof-comparison shortcuts need the fact records and the probes); both.}
\li{\strong{Run line.} \code{\verb~|rlvm[-jvm] --threads 8 --stats -X engine=machine -X passes=N FILE~}: mode reload (every pass is a complete run), and the load defaults of this \code{\verb~|rlvm-main~}: \code{\verb~|load=pipelined(auto) scan-threads=2(auto) io=auto~} (mapped input, two scanner threads beside the loader thread; the integrator's 27.4 s / 21.2 s were taken with one buffered reader thread - LP2 measured the two forms within the spread between rows).}
\li{\strong{JVM launcher flags.} As shipped: \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=2 -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow -Xms13g -Xmx13g -Xmn1g~} (cedar: \code{\verb~|-Xms6g -Xmx6g -Xmn1g~}, the host's memory rule). J1's line: \code{\verb~|-XX:+UseSerialGC -XX:-UseJVMCICompiler -XX:CICompilerCount=8 -XX:+UseTransparentHugePages -Xlog:disable -Xlog:all=warning:stderr -XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError --enable-native-access=ALL-UNNAMED --sun-misc-unsafe-memory-access=allow -Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}.}
\li{\strong{Host.} aarch64, 10 Cortex-X925 + 10 Cortex-A725, 121 GB, shared with about ten agents (load average 40-65 all evening). Every number in seconds below was taken inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window on the 8 measurement cores (X925: 5-9, 15-17), one Mathlib process per window (\code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/w/*.sh~}; the window log is \code{\verb~|work/perf-history/quiet.log~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.2 Times and shares come from different passes of the same process}

\p{A profiler distorts times. So:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Seconds} are from UNPROFILED passes: wall clock of the pass line (\code{\verb~|rlvm: pass K/N ..~}), CPU seconds per thread from \code{\verb~|/proc/PID/task/TID/schedstat~} (on-CPU and run-queue nanoseconds; \code{\verb~|stat~} for kernel time and faults), polled every 100 ms (cedar: 10 ms) by \code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/tools/run.py~} from a core outside the window's eight and interpolated at the pass boundaries; collections from \code{\verb~|-Xlog:gc,safepoint~} (JVM) and \code{\verb~|-XX:+PrintGC~} (image).}
\li{\strong{Shares} are from a LATER pass of the SAME process, to which the profiler is attached when the line of the last unprofiled pass has been read: \code{\verb~|jcmd PID JFR.start~} on the JVM (the process ran with \code{\verb~|-XX:+UnlockDiagnosticVMOptions -XX:+DebugNonSafepoints -XX:FlightRecorderOptions:stackdepth=1024~} from its start: these change no generated code - on cedar the process with them is at 1.63-1.65 s in passes 5-6, the one without at 1.66-1.67 s in passes 7-8), \code{\verb~|perf record -p PID~} in the image.}
\li{An item's CPU seconds = its share of the worker samples of the profiled pass x the workers' CPU seconds of the unprofiled pass. Its wall-clock seconds = CPU seconds / 8: the pass runs 8 workers on 8 cores, and level 1 shows that the cores are busy for all but a few percent of the pass, so a CPU second anywhere costs an eighth of a second of wall clock.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.3 The samples}

\ul{
\li{\strong{JVM: \code{\verb~|jdk.CPUTimeSample~}} (JEP 509, JDK 25; \code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/tools/p10.jfc~}): a per-thread CPU-time timer of 2 ms, so every thread is sampled in proportion to the CPU it uses (the classic \code{\verb~|jdk.ExecutionSample~} takes a few threads per wall-clock period; recorded too, as a cross-check). With \code{\verb~|DebugNonSafepoints~} a sample names the interrupted pc's whole inlining chain, and the stack below it. Host recursion of the machine reaches depth 128 before a judgment is suspended to the machine's own stack, so the Java stack carries the judgments that are in progress (up to 1,024 frames are recorded; 8 of the 78,186 Mathlib stacks were cut at that depth).}
\li{\strong{Native image: \code{\verb~|perf record~}} inside the arena container (\code{\verb~|bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits~}), events of the X925 PMU (\code{\verb~|armv8_pmuv3_1~}) with fixed periods: \code{\verb~|cpu-clock~} 1 ms, \code{\verb~|cpu_cycles~} 8,000,009, \code{\verb~|inst_retired~} 20,000,003, \code{\verb~|l1d_cache_refill~} 80,021, \code{\verb~|l2d_cache_refill~} 60,013, \code{\verb~|ll_cache_miss_rd~} 12,007, \code{\verb~|br_mis_pred~} 80,021, \code{\verb~|dtlb_walk~} 2,503, \code{\verb~|stall_backend_mem~} 2,000,003 (about 4,700 samples per thread-second in all). No call chains (the image keeps no frame pointer); every sampled address is resolved into its chain of INLINED frames with the DWARF info of \code{\verb~|dist-sym~} (\code{\verb~|gdb~}'s block table; \code{\verb~|tools/perf-items.py~}).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.4 One item per method: \code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/items.map~}}

\p{A text file of rules \code{\verb~|KIND ITEM fully.qualified.Class#method~} (first match wins), read by both classifiers (\code{\verb~|tools/JfrItems.java~}, \code{\verb~|tools/perf-items.py~}):}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|item~}: the INNERMOST frame of a sample that has an item names the sample's item ("what is being done"). \code{\verb~|Hash.record~} inlined into \code{\verb~|Values.closureProbe~} inlined into \code{\verb~|Eval.app~} is hashing; the \code{\verb~|Terms.kind~} read inlined into \code{\verb~|Infer.twalk~} is a term-store read.}
\li{\code{\verb~|skip~}: no item of its own - the sample goes to the next outer frame: JDK code, the one-line record readers (\code{\verb~|Values.kind~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.arg~} ..), the static forwarders of \code{\verb~|Ops~}.}
\li{\code{\verb~|ctx~}: a context that wins over the innermost frame when it is anywhere on the stack: the reference-engine fallback (\code{\verb~|rlvm.ref.*~}, \code{\verb~|Worker.reference~}: "and everything it calls in the store"), and the decoding of a unit's proof block (\code{\verb~|ProofBlocks.decode~} and the overlay nodes it writes).}
\li{Second column of every table, "by compile unit": the item of the OUTERMOST frame of the sampled frame's inlining chain, i.e. of the compiled method the pc is in - who asked. It is what a symbol-level profile sees, and the gap between the two columns is what inlining hides from one.}
\li{\code{\verb~|incl NAME~}: markers for inclusive counts (samples with a judgment anywhere on the host stack), used for proof irrelevance, the K rule, structure eta.}
}

\p{To re-make the table after a change: run a window script, then \code{\verb~|tools/post-jvm.sh~} / \code{\verb~|tools/post-native.sh~}; the classifiers print the methods that no rule names.}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.5 Error bars}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Wall clock}: two later passes of one process differ by up to 6 \% (21.93 and 23.27 s with the same CPU: the difference is core time that other processes of the loaded host took on the measurement cores - the process's threads were runnable and waiting meanwhile, level 1 shows it as its own line). Worker CPU of two later passes: within 1 \%.}
\li{\strong{Sampling}: 78,186 worker samples (JVM, Mathlib) and 184,544 (image): a 10 \% line has a standard error of 0.1 point, a 1 \% line of 0.04 point. Recorder losses: 0.3 \% of the JVM samples; the samples x 2 ms cover 99.2 \% of the workers' CPU of the profiled pass.}
\li{\strong{JVM, where the pc is}: 95.9 \% of the Mathlib samples are taken at the interrupted pc, 4.1 \% at the next safepoint ("biased"). The exact samples alone give every group within 0.4 point of the table; the classic sampler (\code{\verb~|jdk.ExecutionSample~}, 20,614 samples) within 0.8 point (fallback 13.3 against 12.5 \%).}
\li{\strong{Inlined frames}: the innermost-frame column depends on the compiler's debug information for inlined one-line readers. On the JVM it is exact to the instruction (\code{\verb~|DebugNonSafepoints~}); in the image the readers of \code{\verb~|Terms~} are attributed to their callers more often (term-store reads 2.9 \% against 7.2 \%, with conversion and inference correspondingly higher: groups 3 + 6 + 7 together are 31.3 \% on the JVM and 29.7 \% in the image). Between neighbouring lines of that kind the two platforms differ by up to 4 points; the groups that have their own loops (hash consing, memo, evaluation, levels, fallback) agree within 2.5 points.}
\li{\strong{The profiled pass is slower} (JFR: +5.4 \% worker CPU, +7.5 \% wall on Mathlib; perf: +2.9 \% and +2.8 \%). The shares are of the profiled pass; the seconds are of the unprofiled one. If the recorder's overhead is not proportional (it is paid per sample, i.e. proportionally to CPU time), a line can be off by a fraction of that.}
\li{\strong{Image: no stacks.} A native sample has no caller. The reference fallback's use of the store (substitution, node reads) is split off with the caller split measured on the JVM for the same corpus and switch set (the fragment "Caller split" in section 3.2 lists the fractions): the image's fallback line is an estimate to that extent (its own code, \code{\verb~|rlvm.ref~}, is 3.1 \% measured; 11.1 \% with the split).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Mathlib on the JVM launcher (as shipped)}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 Level 1: where the 8 cores are}

\p{\strong{Mathlib, JVM launcher as shipped (13 GB heap), machine default set} - pass 2: wall clock \strong{21.93 s}; process CPU 164.06 s (the pass line: 164.36 s); 8 cores x wall = 175.4 core-seconds}

\table{
\tr{\th{where the 8 cores are}\th{CPU s}\th{= wall s (CPU / 8)}\th{share of the pass}\th{runnable without a CPU, s}\th{of the CPU: kernel s}\th{minor faults}}
\tr{\td{workers (8 threads)}\td{148.32}\td{18.54}\td{84.5 \%}\td{21.03}\td{0.04}\td{407}}
\tr{\td{loader thread (reader, linking, loader, store building)}\td{5.90}\td{0.74}\td{3.4 \%}\td{5.28}\td{0.03}\td{10}}
\tr{\td{scanner threads (2)}\td{5.90}\td{0.74}\td{3.4 \%}\td{7.53}\td{0.28}\td{95,316}}
\tr{\td{environment thread (admission of inductive blocks)}\td{1.33}\td{0.17}\td{0.8 \%}\td{1.47}\td{0.00}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{JIT compiler threads (C1 + C2)}\td{2.03}\td{0.25}\td{1.2 \%}\td{0.83}\td{0.01}\td{1,796}}
\tr{\td{collector (VM thread)}\td{0.58}\td{0.07}\td{0.3 \%}\td{0.06}\td{0.08}\td{85,264}}
\tr{\td{other threads}\td{0.00}\td{0.00}\td{0.0 \%}\td{0.03}\td{0.00}\td{0}}
\tr{\td{idle cores while a collection has stopped the threads (7 x 0.56 s of pauses)}\td{-}\td{0.49}\td{2.2 \%}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{cores the process did not use otherwise: taken by other processes of the host, or idle (its threads waited 36.2 s in run queues meanwhile)}\td{-}\td{0.93}\td{4.2 \%}\td{36.23}\td{}\td{}}
\tr{\td{\strong{sum}}\td{\strong{164.06}}\td{\strong{21.93}}\td{100 \%}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
}

\p{Workers' account (8 threads x their life in the pass = 173.6 s): on a CPU 148.32 s (85.4 \%), runnable without a CPU 21.03 s (12.1 \%), stopped for collections 4.50 s (2.6 \%), parked / other blocked -0.24 s (-0.1 \%); not yet started or already ended 1.82 s.}

\p{Collections in the pass: 26 young + 0 full, 0.56 s of pauses (longest 43 ms), 14.9 GB freed; the pass line's own count: 26 collections, 563 ms; safepoints 26, 0.59 s in all (reaching them 0.003 s; other than collections 0.000 s: none).}

\ul{
\li{The workers are on a CPU for 85 \% of their life, runnable without one for 12 \%, stopped by collections for 2.6 \% and parked for nothing: no worker waits for the loader, the frontier or a lock (P7's finding stands). The run-queue time is the price of 12 runnable threads on 8 cores while the file loads (the loader side uses 13.1 s of CPU) plus what the host takes.}
\li{Pass 3 of the same process: 23.27 s with 163.6 s of CPU (workers 149.5 s); 2.34 s of it are cores the process did not get (10.1 \% against 4.2 \% in pass 2). The checker's own account is the same in both passes.}
\li{The workers' account is taken from the same kernel counters as the driver's own (\code{\verb~|-X stats-file~}, \src{rlvm-driver/bin/breakdown.py}), read from outside so that the timed process runs without statistics. Cross-check on cedar, where a statistics run was in the window (pass 8, 1.61 s): the driver says on a CPU 81.5 \%, run queue 13.8 \%, blocked (collections, faults) 4.1 \%, parked 0.4 \% of workers x check wall; the poller says 10.27 s on a CPU and 1.75 s in the run queue of 12.6 s: 81.5 \% and 13.9 \%.}
\li{The loader side, by item (same samples):}
\li{loader thread: 5.90 s CPU, 2853 samples: store.blocks 32.3 \% (1.90 s), load.loader 16.2 \% (0.96 s), store.decls 13.5 \% (0.79 s), store.read 12.3 \% (0.72 s), store.subst 11.4 \% (0.67 s), load.link 5.9 \% (0.35 s), level 5.4 \% (0.32 s), load.scan 2.9 \% (0.17 s), num 0.1 \% (0.01 s), driver 0.1 \% (0.01 s)}
\li{scanner threads: 5.90 s CPU, 2743 samples: load.scan 80.5 \% (4.75 s), load.link 19.5 \% (1.15 s)}
\li{environment thread: 1.33 s CPU, 656 samples: ref 63.1 \% (0.84 s), store.subst 14.3 \% (0.19 s), store.read 7.6 \% (0.10 s), admit 6.9 \% (0.09 s), driver 3.5 \% (0.05 s), store.decls 2.9 \% (0.04 s), level 1.4 \% (0.02 s), store.blocks 0.3 \% (0.00 s)}
}
}

