\p{Workers' account (8 threads x their life in the pass = 216.0 s): on a CPU 178.83 s (82.8 \%), runnable without a CPU 16.93 s (7.8 \%), stopped for collections 18.85 s (8.7 \%), parked / other blocked 1.39 s (0.6 \%); not yet started or already ended 1.62 s.}

\p{Collections in the pass: 161 young + 3 full, 2.36 s of pauses (longest 149 ms), 21.2 GB freed.}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The collector is the image's own line.} 121 collections per pass (118 young, 3 full), 2.18 s of pauses, 20.9 GB freed. The workers are stopped for 8.3 \% of their life (JVM: 2.6 \%) and seven cores idle meanwhile: 1.91 s of the 26.48 s. The image collects once per 170 MB, the launcher's 1 GB nursery once per 570 MB; and the image's pass frees 20.9 GB where the JVM's frees 14.9 GB (why the image allocates 6 GB more was not looked into).}
\li{\strong{Kernel time} is 7.7 s of the process's CPU (the workers 5.9 s, the loader thread 1.0 s): 6.3 million minor faults per pass, i.e. 24 GB of pages touched for the first time - every allocated byte lands on a fresh page, because the image's collector hands freed chunks back and maps new ones. In the worker samples the kernel is 3.7 \% with an IPC of 1.0 and 10 \% of all last-level cache misses.}
\li{The workers' account of the image (on a CPU 83.3 \%, run queue 7.8 \%, stopped for collections 8.3 \%, parked 0.6 \%) is P7's of the afternoon with other jars (85.6 \%, 6.2 \%, 7.8 \%, 0.3 \%): the driver's side has not changed, the collector's share is the same.}
\li{No JIT threads, no collector thread: the image collects on the thread whose allocation fails, so the collector's own CPU is inside the workers' and the loader's lines (collector code 0.2 \%, its copies below 0.4 \%; the rest of a pause is waiting for the threads to stop, and kernel work).}
}
}

