}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Hash consing is not where the cache misses are.} It has 25.4 \% of the CPU time, 26.6 \% of the instructions, 27.1 \% of the L1 misses and 22.9 \% of the last-level read misses: average. Its probes of closures, frames and neutral applications run at an IPC of 4.2-4.4. Sessions are reset at a 1 MiB cap (17,510 of the 53,966 resets of a pass), so their tables stay cache-sized.}
\li{\strong{The cache-miss items} are: universe levels (3.0 \% of the instructions, 11.7 \% of the last-level misses, 17.3 \% of the TLB walks, IPC 1.77: the level store and its list store are shared, large and probed by hash); table growth and re-hash (IPC 2.1); the kernel (IPC 1.0); the probes of constants (\code{\verb~|mkConst~}, 31 \% of its cycles stalled) and the code blocks' inline caches and lookups (35 \%); the pair table (0.83 last-level misses per 1,000 instructions, 2.6 times the average).}
\li{\strong{Branches}: 2.1 mispredictions per 1,000 instructions overall; hashing-and-compare loops (table growth 6.3, hashing 7.1) are the outliers, but small.}
}

\p{The other threads of the image, by item (same pass):}

\ul{
\li{loader thread: 7.75 s CPU, 7962 samples: store.blocks 23.0 \% (1.78 s), kernel 16.1 \% (1.25 s), load.loader 14.7 \% (1.14 s), store.read 11.7 \% (0.91 s), store.decls 10.4 \% (0.81 s), store.subst 9.4 \% (0.73 s), load.link 5.0 \% (0.39 s), level 3.4 \% (0.26 s), load.scan 2.7 \% (0.21 s), runtime.copy 2.5 \% (0.19 s), gc.collect 0.7 \% (0.05 s), num 0.3 \% (0.02 s)}
\li{scanner threads: 7.75 s CPU, 7766 samples: load.scan 63.0 \% (4.89 s), runtime 11.6 \% (0.90 s), load.link 8.9 \% (0.69 s), runtime.copy 8.1 \% (0.63 s), kernel 7.9 \% (0.61 s), gc.collect 0.4 \% (0.03 s), libc 0.1 \% (0.00 s), runtime.safepoint 0.0 \% (0.00 s), gc.alloc 0.0 \% (0.00 s), image-no-symbol 0.0 \% (0.00 s)}
\li{environment thread: 1.88 s CPU, 1936 samples: store.subst 38.6 \% (0.72 s), ref 18.6 \% (0.35 s), kernel 13.1 \% (0.25 s), store.read 9.4 \% (0.18 s), admit 8.2 \% (0.15 s), level 5.0 \% (0.09 s), driver 2.5 \% (0.05 s), runtime.copy 2.1 \% (0.04 s), gc.collect 1.2 \% (0.02 s), store.decls 1.1 \% (0.02 s), runtime 0.2 \% (0.00 s), libc 0.1 \% (0.00 s)}
\li{The scanner threads need 7.8 s in the image against 5.9 s on the JVM, and 17 \% of their samples are one 8-byte read: \code{\verb~|rlvm.export.Mem.u64~} (a \code{\verb~|byteArrayViewVarHandle~}) 8.9 \% plus the method-handle plumbing the image runs for it (\code{\verb~|Util_java_lang_invoke_MethodHandle.convertArgs~}, \code{\verb~|linkTo~}, a reflection accessor: 8.5 \%, the "runtime" entry above). HotSpot compiles the same call to a load. That is 1.3 s of CPU per pass (0.16 s of wall clock) which only the image pays.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 The two platforms, item by item; AR1's instruction view}

\p{Where the 4.55 s between the image (26.48 s) and the JVM launcher (21.93 s) are, from the level-1 lines:}

\table{
\tr{\th{}\th{image - JVM, CPU s}\th{wall s}}
\tr{\td{workers, user code: the image's code needs 14 \% more CPU than C2's (169.4 against 148.3 s)}\td{+21.1}\td{+2.63}}
\tr{\td{workers, kernel: page faults (5.1 M against 407)}\td{+5.9}\td{+0.74}}
\tr{\td{loader thread, scanner threads, environment thread (of it kernel +1.5 s)}\td{+4.0}\td{+0.50}}
\tr{\td{collections: cores idle during the pauses (2.18 s of pauses against 0.56 s)}\td{}\td{+1.42}}
\tr{\td{JIT compiler threads and collector thread (the JVM's own)}\td{-2.6}\td{-0.33}}
\tr{\td{cores the host took (the JVM's pass lost more)}\td{}\td{-0.41}}
\tr{\td{\strong{sum}}\td{}\td{\strong{+4.55}}}
}

\p{So half of the gap is memory management (pauses 1.4 s + page faults 0.9 s of wall clock) and half is code quality. The first half is the image's collector and its configuration, not the checker: the JVM launcher's serial collector has a 1 GB nursery on a heap that is committed and touched once.}

\p{Item by item the two platforms tell the same story, with these differences (shares of the workers' CPU, JVM / image): hash consing 27.6 / 25.4 \%, memo lookups 11.4 / 10.0 \%, evaluation + code + reduction 16.4 / 17.5 \%, universe levels 7.9 / 6.2 \%, the fallback 12.5 / 11.1 \%, numeric 0.1 / 0.2 \%; and three lines that trade places because the image's debug information attributes inlined \code{\verb~|Terms~} readers to their callers: term store 11.4 / 5.7 \%, conversion 4.4 / 7.3 \%, inference without levels 7.7 / 10.5 \% (together 23.5 / 23.5 \%). The image alone has the kernel line (3.7 \%), collector code on worker threads (0.2 \%), array-copy stubs without a caller (0.4 \%), and a visible worklist line (0.4 \% against 0.02 \%: \code{\verb~|tickSlow~}, frame pushes that C2 inlines away). In CPU seconds the image's lines are larger where the work is the machine's own loops - hash consing 44.5 against 40.9 s, evaluation and code 30.6 against 24.4 s, memo lookups 17.6 against 16.8 s - and the same where it is bound by memory: universe levels 10.9 against 11.7 s, the fallback 19.4 against 18.6 s, proof-block decoding 4.1 against 4.3 s. C2 buys its 14 \% in the instruction-bound items.}

\p{Cross-check with AR1 6.2 (instructions by class of the symbol, all threads; AR1: \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, 4 threads, a cold run, 2,982 G sampled; here: today's default set, 8 workers, the profiled warm pass):}

\pre{\verb~|
instructions sampled: 2859.2 G in the profiled pass(es)
by thread: worker 92.7 %, scan 4.1 %, loader 2.3 %, env 0.9 %
by module: rlvm-machine 73.8 %, rlvm-store 15.0 %, rlvm-export + rlvm-load 3.6 %, rlvm-ref 3.0 %, other runtime, JDK, libc, no symbol 1.6 %, kernel 1.4 %, rlvm-level, rlvm-api 1.4 %, other rlvm (inductive) 0.1 %, garbage collector (genscavenge) 0.1 %, other rlvm (driver) 0.0 %, other rlvm (admit) 0.0 %
by class of the symbol: `Values` 19.0 %, `Eval` 16.6 %, `Infer` 11.3 %, `Code` 6.9 %, `Same` 4.4 %, `Terms` 4.1 %, `Overlay` 3.9 %, `Conv` 3.9 %, `Runs` 3.5 %, `TermOps$Rewriter` 2.8 %, `Reduce` 2.0 %, `RefKernel` 1.7 %, `Machine` 1.6 %, `Levels` 1.6 %, `<kernel>` 1.4 %, `Scanner` 1.3 %, `IntIntMap` 1.3 %, `NodeCtx` 1.1 %, `TermOps$Memo` 1.0 %, `Pairs` 1.0 %, `LevelStore` 0.9 %, `RunPacker` 0.9 %, `CodeStore` 0.9 %, `ProofBlocks` 0.8 %
~}

\p{AR1 found \code{\verb~|Values~} 28.7 \%, \code{\verb~|Eval~} 26.1 \%, \code{\verb~|Infer~} 9.2 \%, the store 12.7 \%, the loader thread 4.5 \%, the collector 0.0 \% of the instructions. Today: \code{\verb~|Values~} 19.0 \%, \code{\verb~|Eval~} 16.6 \%, \code{\verb~|Infer~} 11.3 \%, \code{\verb~|Code~} 6.9 \%, \code{\verb~|Same~} 4.4 \%, the store 15.0 \%, loader + scanner threads 6.4 \%, the collector 0.1 \%, the kernel 1.4 \%. The two views agree where they can: the collector executes almost no instructions (its cost is the pause and the page faults, which an instruction count of user code does not see - AR1's "collector 0.0 \%" is true and is not the collector's cost); the store's share is the fallback plus the node reads. They differ by what the switch set of 19:41Z changed: \code{\verb~|bodies~}, \code{\verb~|code~}, \code{\verb~|casetrees~}, \code{\verb~|lazyeval~}, \code{\verb~|same~} and \code{\verb~|match~} moved work out of \code{\verb~|Eval~} and \code{\verb~|Values~} into \code{\verb~|Code~}, \code{\verb~|Same~} and \code{\verb~|Infer~}. Class-level shares are the "by compile unit" column of this report; the item column cuts across classes (\code{\verb~|Values~} is 19 \% of the instructions, hash consing 26.6 \%: the other 7 points are \code{\verb~|Runs~}, \code{\verb~|Hash~} and \code{\verb~|Machine~}'s table code).}
}
}

