\p{Event counts of the same four configurations (statistics runs of the shipped image, counts only; default / (a) / (b) / (c)): ticks 1,247.9 M / 1,302.8 M / 528.2 M / 575.9 M; PIRR calls 38.5 M / 38.9 M / 1.5 M / 1.5 M; VTYPE calls 72.7 M / 73.1 M / 18.2 M / 18.2 M; units re-checked by the reference engine 6,799 / 88 / 6,771 / 60; Nat folds 273,045 / 817,989 / 264,759 / 792,570.}

\p{Reading:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{(a) \code{\verb~|eager~}: JVM 21.93 -> 18.07 s (-18 \%), 164.1 -> 140.7 s of CPU; image 27.20 -> 24.18 s (-11 \%), 196.2 -> 179.6 s.} The fallback line goes from 18.6 s to 1.2 s (image 19.8 -> 1.2 s): what is left are the 88 units declined at other limits. The machine now checks the 6,711 units itself (+55 M ticks, 4 \% more); in the image that shows as +3.5 s of hash consing and +1.8 s of evaluation, on the JVM every other group stays within 2 s. Half of the pass's garbage was the reference kernel's: JVM 26 -> 15 collections and 14.9 -> 7.5 GB, image 164 -> 128 collections, 2.36 -> 1.34 s of pauses, and the workers' kernel time 6.7 -> 3.0 s. The eager units are also where Mathlib's Nat arithmetic is: the machine's Nat folds triple (0.27 -> 0.82 M), and the numeric line goes from 0.15 to 0.25 s on the JVM (0.40 -> 0.62 s in the image). Still nothing.}
\li{\strong{(b) the two shortcuts: JVM 21.93 -> 15.71 s (-28 \%), 164.1 -> 119.5 s of CPU; image 27.20 -> 20.40 s (-25 \%), 196.2 -> 146.8 s.} The workers need 43.1 s less on the JVM (49.4 s in the image); 58 \% of the ticks and 96 \% of the PIRR calls are gone, and proof irrelevance on the stack goes from 27.5 \% (40.8 s) to 1.4 \% (1.5 s). What the rule had been paying for (JVM / image): hash consing 18.8 / 20.6 s, evaluation and code blocks 12.4 / 13.3 s, memo lookups 7.5 / 6.2 s, conversion's own code 1.3 / 4.6 s, VTYPE 1.4 / 2.4 s, node reads 1.0 / 0.7 s, levels 0.9 / 0.5 s. New: 1.5-1.7 s of fact records (\code{\verb~|facts~}). The fallback is untouched (19.4 / 20.2 s) and is then 18 \% of the workers on the JVM.}
\li{\strong{(c) both: JVM 13.05 s (-40 \%), 100.7 s of CPU; image 17.16 s (-37 \%), 124.2 s.} The two savings add up (JVM: 21.2 s + 43.1 s of worker CPU separately, 60.8 s together). The workers' 87.5 s on the JVM are then: hash consing 23.1 s (26 \%), inference 10.9 s + universe levels 9.7 s (24 \%), term store 13.9 s (16 \%), evaluation and code 13.7 s (16 \%), memo lookups 9.9 s (11 \%), conversion 4.7 s (5 \%), everything around the machine 1.6 s (2 \%); collections: 11 per pass, 4.8 GB, 0.28 s (image: 113 collections, 1.35 s).}
\li{\strong{The image stays 4-5 s behind the JVM in every configuration} (27.2 / 21.9, 24.2 / 18.1, 20.4 / 15.7, 17.2 / 13.1 s): the gap is not in the items the switches remove. With (c) its collector is 1.35 s of pauses in a 17.2 s pass.}
\li{\strong{gruszka on the same cores: 12.5 s of wall clock and 75.6 s of CPU with 8 threads} (\code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~}). The default configuration on the JVM launcher is 9.4 s and 88.5 s of CPU above it. Of the 88.5 s the proof comparison is 43 s and the fallback 17-21 s; after both, (c) is 0.6 s and 25.1 s above, and of those 25 s the loader side (loader thread, scanner threads, environment thread: 12.8 s) and universe levels (9.7 s) are the two largest single lines that are not the core of checking. gruszka's CPU includes its own load; how its 75.6 s split is not known to this report.}
}
}
}

