\ul{
\li{cedar's pass is 1.63 s on the JVM launcher (12.4 s of CPU) and 2.04 s in the image (14.5 s); gruszka needs 2.07 s (10.0 s of CPU, \code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~}). The JVM is not steady before pass 5 with \code{\verb~|CICompilerCount=2~} (0.71 s of JIT CPU are still in the timed pass); with J1's line it is at 1.53 s from pass 3 on (section 2.6).}
\li{\strong{The same items in nearly the same proportions, without the proof comparison.} Hash consing 30.6 \% (JVM) / 26.6 \% (image), memo lookups 8.4 \%, term store 17.2 \% (the proofs are smaller and read once: reading the export is 10.9 \%, decoding proof blocks 4.8 \%), evaluation / code / reduction 15.7 \%, conversion 4.3 \%, inference 10.1 \% + levels 3.2 \%, the fallback 9.5 \% (424 declined units of 146,058, 423 of them at eager reduction).}
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance is on the stack of 2.6 \% of the samples} (Mathlib: 27.5 \%). The rule is CALLED 2.16 million times per pass (statistics of the same jar: 67.3 M judgment calls, 85.0 M ticks), so a call costs 0.12 microseconds of CPU with everything it triggers; on Mathlib 38.5 M calls cost 40.8 s, 1.06 microseconds each. That is the difference between the two corpora.}
\li{\strong{Universe levels are 3.2 \%} (Mathlib 7.9 \%).}
\li{\strong{Numeric and literal computation: 0.14 \%} (0.01 s of CPU), 193,986 Nat folds and 3,928 offset steps per pass; with everything a fold triggers (the evaluation of its arguments) 1.5 \% of the samples.}
\li{The K rule is on the stack of 0.8 \%, structure eta of a major premise of 0.4 \%, structure eta in conversion of 0.3 \%, iota of 5.3 \%.}
\li{The image's collector: 20 collections and 0.18 s of pauses per 2.04 s pass (8 \% of the pass with the cores it idles), 0.57 s of kernel time (0.5 M page faults); the JVM: 2 collections, 0.04 s.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What surprised, and what could not be attributed}

\p{What surprised:}

\ol{
\li{\strong{"gc" is two different answers.} On the JVM launcher it is 2.6 \% of the pass and not worth a line. In the image it is the second-largest line of level 1 after the workers: 2.2 s of pauses per pass (121 collections, one per 170 MB, where the launcher's 1 GB nursery makes 26, one per 570 MB), and the kernel time of 6.3 million page faults on top (7.7 s of CPU). AR1's instruction view could not see either.}
\li{\strong{Numeric computation is nothing} (0.1 \%): the owner's third example is the smallest line of the table.}
\li{\strong{Hash consing is big and cache-friendly.} A quarter of the workers' CPU, but at an IPC above 4 with average miss rates: the cost is the number of probes (instructions), not the memory. Making a probe cheaper buys little; making fewer of them is what the shortcuts do (-18.8 s of its 40.9 s).}
\li{\strong{Universe levels are a top-five line} (7.9 \%, and 11 \% of what is left in configuration (c)) and the one real cache-miss item of the machine: every evaluated constant with level parameters instantiates its level list (\code{\verb~|Levels.lsubList~}, a 2^n-entry direct-mapped cache in front) and interns it in the shared store (\code{\verb~|IntListStore.intern~}, \code{\verb~|LevelStore.intern~}).}
\li{\strong{Proof irrelevance costs nothing itself and a quarter of the pass through what it asks for.} No line of a flat profile shows it; the host stack does.}
\li{\strong{0.94 \% of the units are 12.5 \% of the workers' CPU} (the fallback), and 58 \% of that is the term store's substitution machinery, which the machine itself never calls.}
\li{\strong{The measurement cores are not quiet}: between two passes of one process the wall clock moved by 1.3 s (6 \%) with identical CPU; other processes took 4-10 \% of the eight cores inside a window. Level 1 separates it; a single wall-clock number does not.}
\li{\strong{The JVM has nothing left to give by itself}: 99.999 \% compiled frames, 2 deoptimisations, no safepoint other than collections.}
\li{\strong{The code blocks' inline cache for constants (\code{\verb~|Code.kConst~}) is 3.4 \% by itself}: one probe of a table that is indexed by (site, level substitution); in the image 35 \% of the cycles of the inline-cache and block-lookup line are stalled on memory.}
\li{\strong{A \code{\verb~|VarHandle~} that is free on the JVM costs the image's scanner threads 17 \% of their CPU} (\code{\verb~|Mem.u64~}, 1.3 s per pass): the image does not turn the byte-array view handle into a load.}
\li{\strong{The machine tries every declined unit twice} (13,598 declines for 6,799 units) before the reference engine gets it: with \code{\verb~|eager~} both the wasted attempts and the fallback go.}
}

\p{What could not be attributed, or only with a caveat:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Nothing is left over in the sample accounting} (JVM: every worker sample has an item; image: 0.04 \% vdso, 0.4 \% array-copy stubs sampled without a caller, 3.7 \% kernel as one line - the kernel's symbols are not readable in the container, so page faults, \code{\verb~|madvise~} and the clearing of new pages are not separated; the fault counts say it is mostly first-touch faults).}
\li{\strong{The collector's own CPU in the image} is inside the workers' and loader's lines and only partly visible (collector code 0.2 \%, copies below 0.4 \%); the rest of a pause is waiting for all threads to stop, and kernel work. The pause total is exact (\code{\verb~|-XX:+PrintGC~}); its split is not.}
\li{\strong{The fallback's store work in the image} is split off with the JVM's caller ratio (no stacks in the image).}
\li{\strong{What a judgment triggers after it was suspended to the machine's own stack} (5.6 \% of the samples are such continuation steps): the inclusive lines of section 2.3 miss it; the ablation of section 4 does not.}
\li{\strong{Inlined one-line readers in the image}: \code{\verb~|Terms~} reads are partly counted under their callers there (section 1.5). The JVM column is the one to read for the term store.}
\li{\strong{Declarations}: this report is by item, not by declaration (P8 and P9 have that view). For the numeric 0.1 \% the counts say only this much: two thirds of Mathlib's Nat folds (0.55 M of 0.82 M) are in the 6,711 units that enter eager reduction, which the default configuration hands to the reference engine (section 4).}
\li{\strong{JFR against perf on one platform} was not possible (perf does not work on the host, JFR not in the image): the cross-check is across platforms, which mixes the compiler into it.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. Files, and how to re-make the tables}

\p{Everything is under \code{\verb~|work/prof/P10/~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{what}\th{where}}
\tr{\td{the assignment method -> item}\td{\code{\verb~|items.map~} (rules, first match wins; header explains the kinds)}}
\tr{\td{distributions}\td{\code{\verb~|dist/~} (\code{\verb~|BUILD-INFO.txt~}: jars, flags), \code{\verb~|dist-sym/~} (same jars + \code{\verb~|-g~}; \code{\verb~|tools/build-sym.sh~})}}
\tr{\td{window scripts (one \src{bench/quiet.py} window each)}\td{\code{\verb~|w/lib.sh~} (run lines \code{\verb~|jvm~}, \code{\verb~|jvmj1~}, \code{\verb~|native~}, \code{\verb~|nperf~}), \code{\verb~|w/one.sh~}, \code{\verb~|w/q.sh~} (slot, window, log), \code{\verb~|w/cedar1.sh~}}}
\tr{\td{runner: stamped output, per-thread CPU / run queue / kernel time / faults per pass, profiler hook after pass K}\td{\code{\verb~|tools/run.py~}}}
\tr{\td{JFR settings; classifier of JFR samples}\td{\code{\verb~|tools/p10.jfc~}, \code{\verb~|tools/JfrItems.java~} (\code{\verb~|tools/jfr-items.sh~})}}
\tr{\td{perf in the container; aggregation; address -> inlined frames -> items}\td{\code{\verb~|tools/perf-run.sh~}, \code{\verb~|tools/perf-agg.py~}, \code{\verb~|tools/perf-items.py~}}}
\tr{\td{tables}\td{\code{\verb~|tools/table.py~} (level 1, level 2, inclusive, contexts, hardware events), \code{\verb~|tools/jit.py~} (item 9), \code{\verb~|tools/answer.py~}, \code{\verb~|tools/compare.py~}, \code{\verb~|tools/methods.py~}, \code{\verb~|tools/ar1check.py~}}}
\tr{\td{everything again from the raw files}\td{\code{\verb~|tools/regen.sh~}; this document: \code{\verb~|tools/mkdoc.py doc/P10.tmpl.md docs/research/profiles/P10.md~}}}
\tr{\td{raw files per run}\td{\code{\verb~|data/mathlib/~}, \code{\verb~|data/cedar/~}: \code{\verb~|<run>.out~} (stamped output), \code{\verb~|.threads.json~}, \code{\verb~|.gc.log~}, \code{\verb~|.jit.log~}, \code{\verb~|.jfr~}, \code{\verb~|.perf.json~} + \code{\verb~|.maps~}, \code{\verb~|.items.{json,txt}~}, \code{\verb~|.report.md~}, \code{\verb~|.frag/~}, \code{\verb~|.table.json~}}}
\tr{\td{window logs}\td{\code{\verb~|logs/w-*.log~}; \code{\verb~|work/perf-history/quiet.log~}}}
}

\p{To re-make the table after a change of the machine:}

\pre{\verb~|
scripts/build.sh --published --no-pgo --dist work/prof/P10/dist          # under bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2
bench/slot.sh -c ni -n 2 -- work/prof/P10/tools/build-sym.sh             # the image for perf
O=$PWD/work/prof/P10/data/mathlib; X=$PWD/work/exports/mathlib.ndjson
work/prof/P10/w/q.sh m-jvm-ship-jfr 24 jvm $O/jvm-ship-jfr 100 "" 3 4 $X        # 3 unprofiled passes, JFR on pass 4
work/prof/P10/w/q.sh m-native-perf 24 nperf $O/native-perf 2 3 "" $X           # 2 unprofiled passes, perf on pass 3
work/prof/P10/w/q.sh m-native-plain 24 native $O/native-plain 100 "" 3 $X      # the shipped image on the host: times
work/prof/P10/tools/regen.sh                                              # tables in out/ and data/*/<run>.report.md
~}

\p{A method that no rule names shows up as \code{\verb~|unassigned~} (JVM) or \code{\verb~|runtime~} (image) in \code{\verb~|<run>.items.txt~} with its share; a new class needs one line in \code{\verb~|items.map~}. Level 3 runs: the same lines with \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=..~} appended (\code{\verb~|logs/~} has the exact commands in the first line of every \code{\verb~|.out~}).}
}
